{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 33, :comments ["<b>And they journeyed from the mountain of YHWH a journey of three days.</b> Our Rabbis of blessed memory homiletically interpreted (Shabbat 116a, Tosafot s.v. פורענות) that on that day they turned away from following YHWH and fled from the mountain of YHWH like a child fleeing from school; therefore they fled for fear lest He add more commandments upon them. And Scripture says: see how ungrateful they are, for even at the time of this rebellion, the ark of the covenant of YHWH was journeying before them a journey of three days to seek out for them a resting place. They rebelled in a span of three days, and the ark of the covenant of YHWH did them good in a span of three days. Another matter: since it is written in Megillat Setarim, 'If you leave Me one day, I will leave you two' (Sifrei, Eikev 11:22) — a parable of two people walking away from each other, one walking a mile eastward and one westward: they are found to be two miles apart. Thus Scripture says that they journeyed away from following YHWH and fell back a distance of three days from the Shekhinah; and correspondingly the ark of the covenant of YHWH too journeyed before them to distance itself from them a journey of three days. Thus they were found to be six days' journey apart — all this in order to seek out for them a resting place from the commandments which they had spurned." "And it is nearly to be heard that this is the secret of the inverted nun. For it is between the word 'the camp' and 'and it came to pass when the ark journeyed' — for the last inverted nun alludes to their complaint, 'we remember the fish,' for nun means fish. But the first nun alludes to Israel being compared to fish, whose essential life is in the water; therefore every fish turns its face to swim into the water toward the place of its life, but it does not turn its face toward the shore, for that is called separating from the place of its life. So too it is said of Israel (Genesis 48:16), 'and let them multiply like fish' — that they should be like those fish, for their essential life is in the Torah, which is compared to water, as in the parable that Rabbi Akiva brought to Pappos ben Judah etc. in tractate Berakhot, chapter 'One who sees' (61a). And at a time when Israel flee from the mountain of YHWH like a child fleeing from school, they are like an inverted nun fish, separating from the place of its life and turning its face toward the shore and outward. So too Israel turned their faces from the ark of the covenant of YHWH, the place of their life — and therefore the inverted nun, for it turned its face away from the verse 'and it came to pass when the ark journeyed' and its face toward the word 'the camp' — an allusion to the time when Israel fell back from the ark of the covenant of YHWH and their faces were toward the camp, that is, toward the people. For love of the affairs of this world in which the people of the camp engage, and in order to find favor in the eyes of the camp, a person turns his back to the Shekhinah and directs all the aim of his deeds toward being an important man in the camp, a prince and ruler over them." "And this is the essence of the sin among us that causes the length of the exile, for the multitude have turned their faces from the ark of the covenant of YHWH toward the camp — to the point that even every act of merit from Torah and commandments they do not perform for its own sake, but only in order to adorn themselves with them before people, to find favor in their eyes. And the destroyers of the city are as though they themselves are the destroyed of the city, and everyone knows it to be so, yet no one says 'return' — until YHWH may look down and see, and remove the spirit of harlotry from our midst. And one should not lengthen on this."], :book :numbers, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 10, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:40:30.947840951Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}